course: dictionary
lesson: 4380
title: A word for home
level: primary-6
stars: 3
explain: Habitation is a formal word, and it is linked to the verb inhabit, which means to live in a place. Context helps you work out its meaning. «The house was not fit for human habitation.» The words not fit tell you that people could not live there safely, so habitation means living in a place.
explain: Habitation can also mean a house or a home. «They camped on a remote common far from habitations.» A common is open land that anyone may use, so the camp was far from the open land and far from the houses. Swap in houses and the sentence still makes sense, so habitations means houses.
fact: Habitation means the act of living in a place, or a house or home where people live.
fact: Habitation is formal, so you will meet it most in older stories, law and history.
fact: «Unfit for habitation» means too dangerous or dirty to live in.
fact: «Signs of habitation» are clues that people once lived somewhere, such as fire marks, tools or walls.
fact: In The Wind in the Willows, Badger's home was built on the ruins of an old human habitation.
exercise: «The house was not fit for human habitation.» What does habitation mean here? | *The act of living in a place | A kind of stone used for building | A loud party | A long journey by boat
exercise: Historians find fire marks, old tools and walls. These are «signs of habitation». What do they show? | The land has never been touched | It rained all year | *People once lived there | Only birds lived there
exercise: Which word in «They camped on a remote common far from habitations» means houses? | remote | common | *habitations | camped
test: In «an old human habitation», what is a habitation? | A river bank | An old wall | A field of grass | *A home where people lived
test: A house that is unfit for habitation is | very quiet and cheap | a good place for a picnic | the tallest house in town | *too dangerous or dirty to live in
exam: Jayden says habitation is just a fancy word for a house. Is he right? | Yes, and it is also a type of animal | No, it only means a holiday | *Partly right: it can mean a house or home, but it can also mean the act of living in a place | No, it means a ruined castle
